          "nudge_reason": null,
          "final_text_tail": "with` statements so contexts are closed (via `Context.__exit__` \u2192 `close()`) when they go out of scope:\n- Main context: `with cli.make_context(...) as ctx:`\n- Non-chained sub-context: `with cmd.make_context(...) as sub_ctx:`\n- Chained sub-context: `with cmd.make_context(...) as sub_sub_ctx:`\n\n**Why it works**: `click.File` opens the file in `convert()` and registers `ctx.call_on_close(f.close)` on the context's `_exit_stack`, which only closes when the context is torn down. Previously the completion contexts were never closed, so files stayed open \u2192 `ResourceWarning`. Wrapping them in `with` guarantees cleanup.\n\n**Verification**:\n- Reproduced the leak on the original code (`ResourceWarning: unclosed file` for plain commands, group subcommands, and chained groups when a `File()` option holds a real value) \u2014 all fixed after the change.\n- All tests pass: shell completion / context / options (188), full suite (558 passed, 21 skipped, 1 xfailed). The only failure is a pre-existing, unrelated collection error in `tests/test_basic.py` (pytest deprecation about a `chain` in `parametrize`) that exists on the original code too.\n\n**Assumption noted**: The task lists `src/click/core.py` as modified, but the described solution lives entirely in `shell_completion.py`. No `core.py` change is behaviorally required (the context-manager protocol already works correctly and `shell_complete`/`_resolve_incomplete` only do read-only access on the now-closed context), so I left `core.py` unchanged.",
